Analogy: Ordering Food
REST = Ordering from a menu at a restaurant.
You don't tell the kitchen how to cook — you just say "I want the Butter Chicken" (a resource: /dishes/butter-chicken). The waiter (HTTP) takes your order using a fixed vocabulary: GET (show me the menu), POST (place a new order), PUT (change my order), DELETE (cancel it). Every restaurant in the world understands this protocol — that's the power of REST.
RPC = Calling your friend who's a chef and telling them exactly what to do.
"Hey, cookButterChicken(extraSpicy=true, servings=2)." You're not asking for a "resource" — you're invoking a function, almost like the two of you are speaking the same private code. It's fast, direct, and tailored — but only works if both sides speak that exact language.

Core Technical Difference
Aspect	REST	RPC
Mental model	Resources (nouns)	Actions (verbs)
Interface	Fixed verbs: GET/POST/PUT/DELETE	Custom methods: getUser(), chargeCard()
Coupling	Loose — client/server evolve independently	Tighter — client often needs the exact method signature
Payload	Usually JSON over HTTP	Often binary (Protobuf/Thrift) — smaller, faster
Performance	Slightly heavier (HTTP overhead, text-based JSON)	Leaner, built for high-throughput internal calls
Best for	Public APIs, third-party integrations	Internal microservice-to-microservice calls
Example tech	HTTP + JSON (Richardson Maturity Model)	gRPC, Apache Thrift

The HLD Trade-off 
"It's not REST vs RPC — it's public contract vs internal speed."
	• Choose REST when: 
		○ You're exposing an API to external clients, partners, or a frontend team you don't control.
		○ You need caching (REST plays beautifully with HTTP caching — CDNs, browser cache).
		○ You want discoverability and a stable, long-lived contract (think: Stripe's API, still recognizable years later).
	• Choose RPC (like gRPC) when: 
		○ You're doing service-to-service calls inside your own infrastructure (e.g., Order Service → Inventory Service).
		○ Latency matters — gRPC uses HTTP/2 + Protobuf, which is significantly faster and smaller than JSON over HTTP/1.1.
		○ You need streaming (bi-directional), strong typing, and auto-generated client/server code across languages.
Most large systems use both — REST/GraphQL at the "edge" (public-facing APIs), and gRPC internally between microservices. That hybrid answer is what separates a senior candidate from someone reciting definitions.

"REST optimizes for interoperability and simplicity at the boundary of your system. RPC optimizes for performance and type-safety inside your system. A good architect doesn't pick one — they pick the right tool for the right boundary."


Simple Implementation: REST vs RPC (Java / Spring Boot)
Scenario: Order Service needs to fetch a user's details.

REST Implementation (Spring Boot + HTTP/JSON)
java
// --- Server side: UserController.java ---
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<UserDTO> getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        UserDTO user = userService.findById(id);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(user); // returns JSON
    }
}
// --- Client side: calling it from Order Service ---
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final RestTemplate restTemplate;
    public UserDTO fetchUser(Long userId) {
        String url = "http://user-service/users/" + userId;
        return restTemplate.getForObject(url, UserDTO.class);
    }
}
What's happening: Order Service treats /users/{id} as a resource. It doesn't know or care how UserService fetches the data internally — it just knows "GET this URL, get JSON back." Loosely coupled, human-readable, easy to test with Postman/curl.

RPC Implementation (gRPC + Protobuf)
Step A — Define the contract (user.proto):
protobuf
syntax = "proto3";
service UserService {
    rpc GetUser (UserRequest) returns (UserResponse);
}
message UserRequest {
    int64 id = 1;
}
message UserResponse {
    int64 id = 1;
    string name = 2;
    string email = 3;
}
Step B — Server implementation:
java
@GrpcService
public class UserGrpcService extends UserServiceGrpc.UserServiceImplBase {
    @Override
    public void getUser(UserRequest request, StreamObserver<UserResponse> responseObserver) {
        UserResponse response = UserResponse.newBuilder()
                .setId(request.getId())
                .setName("Sam Verma")
                .setEmail("sam@example.com")
                .build();
        responseObserver.onNext(response);
        responseObserver.onCompleted();
    }
}
Step C — Client (Order Service) calling it like a local method:
java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @GrpcClient("user-service")
    private UserServiceGrpc.UserServiceBlockingStub userStub;
    public UserResponse fetchUser(Long userId) {
        UserRequest request = UserRequest.newBuilder().setId(userId).build();
        return userStub.getUser(request); // feels like calling a normal Java method
    }
}
What's happening: Notice fetchUser() reads like Order Service is calling a local function — userStub.getUser(request) — even though it's crossing a network boundary. That's the RPC illusion: remote calls disguised as local method calls, backed by a strict binary contract (.proto file) instead of a loose JSON blob.


